#Statistic 101#
	1. Introduction to Statistics
		What is Statistics?
		Why Statistic is Important to Data Analytics?
	  		-Understand Characteristics(patterns&distributions), Organize Information, Summarize via Metrics, Support Evidence-Based Decisions:
	2. Descriptive & Inferential Statistics
		Descriptive Statistics - focuses entirely on organizing, summarizing, and presenting the specific data collected. Example: Calculating monthly average sales across active
store branches.
		Inferential Statistics - uses information drawn from a smaller sample to formulate estimates about a whole population. Example: Surveying 100 buyers to infer overall customer
satisfaction.
	3. Fundamental Statistical Concepts
		What?: Essential definitions and building blocks of data organization.
		Data(1 data/value), Data Sets(multi-data), Populations(fix total n#) and Sample(fraction value to population)
		Variables(one column) vs. Observations(one row)
	4. Types of Data
		Qualitative, Quantitative, Discrete, and Continuous Data
	5. Basic Statistical Functions
		Measures of Central Tendency(3)
			Mean (Arithmetic Average) - AVERAGE(B2:B6)
			Median (Middle Value/Position) - MEDIAN(B2:B6)
			Mode & Multimodal Data - MODE(B2:B6)
		Measures of Dispersion(4)
			Range = MAX(B2:B6)-MIN(B2:B6)
			Variance & Standard Deviation - common risk evaluation measures.
				Variance - (VAR.S(B2:B11))
				Standard Deviation - (STDEV.S(B2:B11)) 
			Coefficient of Variation (CV) - (CV = SD/Mean)



